WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.700
A dark stage.

2
00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:08.260
One line pulses like a heartbeat: "So
foul and fair a day I have not seen."

3
00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:12.800
Then a chant, short and
hard, every stress up front:

4
00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.710
"Double, double, toil and trouble."

5
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.350
Then no beat at all: a
loose block of plain talk,

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.620
sprawling across the stage.

7
00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:24.120
One play, three ways of speaking.

8
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.190
Why would Shakespeare need all of them?

9
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.970
In this mission, you'll hear
the da-DUM beat of blank verse:

10
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.970
5 iambs in a line.

11
00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.300
You'll meet prose, rhyming
couplets and the witches' chant,

12
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.090
and ask who uses each one, and when.

13
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.850
And you'll catch the moments when
the rhythm suddenly breaks apart.

14
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.300
Your mission: explain why
Shakespeare changes how people speak.
